WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.975
showntell turns Markdown written by a coding agent into a narrated slide

2
00:00:04.825 --> 00:00:05.825
walkthrough.

3
00:00:05.875 --> 00:00:09.200
We'll follow one walkthrough from the tool call to your speakers.

4
00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.525
There are three parts.

5
00:00:11.475 --> 00:00:14.950
The agent sends Markdown through MCP tools.

6
00:00:14.937 --> 00:00:18.200
A local daemon parses it and voices it with Kokoro.

7
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.550
And the player in your browser plays it back.

8
00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:23.825
Every tool call lands in _append.

9
00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:27.900
On the first call, it polls until the opening is voiced, usually

10
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.450
a fraction of a second.

11
00:00:29.438 --> 00:00:33.150
Then the reply tells the agent to send the link right away.

12
00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:36.625
You start watching while the agent is still writing the body.

13
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.000
Inside the session, the builder parses the new Markdown.

14
00:00:40.988 --> 00:00:47.000
Headings become slides, fenced blocks become visuals, and plain paragraphs become narration.

15
00:00:46.987 --> 00:00:50.750
Then each new paragraph is sent off to be voiced, and subscribers

16
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.575
hear which slides changed.

17
00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:56.463
Problems, like a cue pointing at a missing line, go back to

18
00:00:56.313 --> 00:00:57.975
the agent in the reply.

19
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.325
The narrator is one worker thread with a priority queue.

20
00:01:02.338 --> 00:01:05.937
A cached paragraph returns at once: the key is a hash of

21
00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:10.975
the model, voice, speed and spoken text, so nothing is voiced twice.

22
00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:14.600
Two sessions asking for the same audio share one job.

23
00:01:14.613 --> 00:01:18.287
Otherwise the job is queued by its position in the walkthrough, so

24
00:01:18.137 --> 00:01:20.750
earlier slides are always voiced first.

25
00:01:21.725 --> 00:01:26.488
The voice is Kokoro, an open eighty two million parameter model, running

26
00:01:26.338 --> 00:01:27.800
on your own machine.

27
00:01:27.775 --> 00:01:32.438
On Apple silicon, auto picks MLX; everywhere else it falls back to

28
00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:33.675
ONNX Runtime.

29
00:01:33.625 --> 00:01:36.125
There's also a fake backend for tests.

30
00:01:36.125 --> 00:01:39.900
Both real backends return the samples plus a start and end time

31
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.000
for every word.

32
00:01:41.975 --> 00:01:44.600
Those word times are what move the pointer.

33
00:01:44.613 --> 00:01:48.350
Each cue is a Markdown link in the narration, at a character

34
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.250
offset.

35
00:01:49.262 --> 00:01:52.950
cue_time finds the first word at or after that offset.

36
00:01:52.925 --> 00:01:56.300
If that word starts a new sentence, the pointer moves a little

37
00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.525
early, into the pause, so your eyes arrive before the voice does.

38
00:02:01.475 --> 00:02:04.425
The player opens a server sent event stream.

39
00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:06.425
It first gets the whole walkthrough.

40
00:02:06.488 --> 00:02:10.813
After that, bursts of changes are merged, and only the changed slides

41
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:11.625
are sent.

42
00:02:11.612 --> 00:02:15.125
So a slide whose audio just finished arrives on its own.

43
00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:19.413
In the browser, build lays every paragraph end to end on a

44
00:02:19.263 --> 00:02:20.775
single timeline.

45
00:02:20.775 --> 00:02:25.287
Voiced paragraphs use their real duration; unvoiced ones get an estimate from

46
00:02:25.137 --> 00:02:26.425
the word count.

47
00:02:26.438 --> 00:02:30.325
A segment is ready once its audio exists, and the player waits

48
00:02:30.175 --> 00:02:32.300
there if it catches up with the writer.

49
00:02:32.337 --> 00:02:36.562
Everything on screen, the slide, the pointer and the caption, is a

50
00:02:36.412 --> 00:02:38.675
pure function of that clock time.

51
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.850
To recap: the server gets the link out early.

52
00:02:42.837 --> 00:02:46.250
The builder turns Markdown into slides and checks them.

53
00:02:46.225 --> 00:02:49.775
The narrator voices each paragraph locally with Kokoro.

54
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.600
And the player puts it together, word by word.
